Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests Utilities list;
register(JitLadder);
define
    cmA;
    cmX;
    cmY;
    cmB;
    cmC;
    cmR;
    cmS;
    cmTwice code={
        if cmA < 0;     cmX = 1;
        if cmA == 0;    cmY = 1;
        };
    cmStore code={
        cmB = cmA < 0;
        cmC = cmA;
        };
    cmCallee code={
        cmR = argument;
        return argument;
        };
    cmInl code={
        cmA = 7;
        cmS = cmCallee(cmA);
        };
    cmDrive code={
        cmTwice();
        cmStore();
        cmInl();
        };
    ;
cmA = 3; cmX = 5; cmY = 5; cmB = 9; cmC = 9; cmR = 9; cmS = 9;
testing(cmDrive);
cerr "CM jitted      twice " cmX "/" cmY " store " cmB "/" cmC " inl " cmR "/" cmS:;
cmA = 3; cmX = 5; cmY = 5; cmB = 9; cmC = 9; cmR = 9; cmS = 9;
cmDrive();
cerr "CM interpreted twice " cmX "/" cmY " store " cmB "/" cmC " inl " cmR "/" cmS:;
cerr "JITCMPOPERAND SENTINEL":;
stop();

jitCmpOperandT -- SEQ 376 step 1 (Tony, R0 / R1 a / R2): does a compare's result land on its operand without recursion?
cmA = 3, so neither compare in cmTwice is true and cmX / cmY stay 5 on both roads. A plain FIELD compared twice does not
show it -- each occurrence of cmA is its own node, so each seeds its own load (measured 2026-10-17). cmStore stores a
compare: jitted it stores the i1 into cmB's count (0), interpreted cmB is data-less and prints its tag -- a disagreement
that is NOT the operand overwrite, pinned as context. cmInl is R0's measurement: an INLINED callee's argument reads the
caller's RUN-TIME value -- cmA is set to 7 inside the jitted body, after the build saw 3, and cmR / cmS must read 7.
The overwrite itself (an argument read twice) aborts the build, so it lives in its own process: jitCmpOperandAT.
